\section*{I. INTRODUCTION}

Representing the potential energy surface of a system is the very first step in performing a molecular dynamics (MD) or Monte Carlo simulation and a challenging task due to the complex manybody nature of real molecular systems. For widely studied systems such as water, this is illustrated by the many models that currently exist. On the one hand, \(a b\) initio studies of water \({ }^{1-6}\) address the modeling of this intricate interplay of interactions but require a high computational expense. On the other hand, classical models \({ }^{7-13}\) offer the advantage of allowing longer simulations and larger system sizes but often lack in transferability and chemical reactivity. In recent years, the advancement of machine learning (ML) techniques has significantly augmented molecular simulations studies, from accelerating the exploration of phase space \({ }^{14,15}\) to enhancing predictions of thermodynamic and spectroscopic properties. \({ }^{16,17}\) Moreover, ML techniques have been proposed to aid the search for improved molecular models of various chemical systems, \({ }^{18-34}\)
such as water. \({ }^{35-43}\) The great advantage of ML models is their ability to combine the \(a b\) initio based accuracy with the computational efficiency of classical force-fields, allowing for first-principles based simulations at larger timescale and length-scales. However, a fundamental shortcoming of many ML models is the limitation of interactions within a localized atom-centered spherical representation with a pre-defined radial cutoff. Thus, long-range interactions present in the training data are not explicitly represented. The resulting models incorporate long-range contributions implicitly, as contributions to the energy resulting from interactions within the cutoff distance. This is an inherent inconsistency that, as we will show here, has implications in the quality of representation of the original system by the ML model. Such an approximation certainly raises concerns given the significance of explicit long-range interactions in electrolyte solutions and melts, \({ }^{44}\) polarized systems, \({ }^{45}\) and generally heterogeneous environments. Nonetheless, many studies using localized ML models have successfully predicted accurate properties for water and other chemical systems. \({ }^{18,24,46}\) In this work, we
explore in detail the shortcomings and sufficiency of localized representations, the influence of the treatment of long-range interactions in ML models, and the properties and phase space where they are necessary.

In recent years, different methodologies have been proposed to tackle the lack of long-range interactions in many ML models. \({ }^{47-53}\) One common approach involves distinguishing between the many-body energy present in the total energy and a classical electrostatic contribution, which is inherently long-ranged. Following this approach, Deng et al. \({ }^{47}\) constructed the electrostatic Spectral Neighbor Analysis Potential (eSNAP), capable of accurately predicting properties of ionic \(\alpha-\mathrm{Li}_{3} \mathrm{~N}\) such as phonon dispersion curves. A more general but also more computationally expensive alternative is to use neural networks trained on data from electronic structure calculations to construct environment-dependent atomic charges, as demonstrated by Artrith et al. \({ }^{48}\) and Yao et al. \({ }^{49}\) More recently, Grisafi and Ceriotti \({ }^{52}\) proposed another approach to tackle this matter-the long distance equivariant representation, which consists of a general form of long-range functionals that could be applied on top of conventional short-ranged ML models. The inclusion of this representation significantly improves the performance of the smooth overlap of atomic positions based models for ion-pair potential energy surfaces and the dielectric response of water. Despite the aforementioned contributions, it remains unclear how the explicit inclusion of long-range interactions to ML models affects their prediction of large-scale bulk thermodynamic quantities.

In this work, we utilize a decomposition formalism similar to the ones employed by Deng et al., \({ }^{47}\) Bartók et al., \({ }^{19}\) and Artrith et al. \({ }^{48}\) to incorporate long-range interactions within the Deep Potential Molecular Dynamics (DeePMD) \({ }^{24,25}\) scheme and show the effects on property predictions for a simple classical model of water. Although the eventual aim is to integrate long-range interactions with ML models trained on energies and forces derived from \(a b\) initio methods, a classical reference model provides a computationally inexpensive avenue to focus the study on the role of longrange interactions in bulk thermodynamic properties. Such a system allows for objective benchmark comparisons due to precisely known pairwise interactions and charge assignments. For these reasons, the model selected in this study is a flexible version of the standard and widely used Extended Simple Point Charge (SPC/E) water model, denoted here as "SPC/E-FW." Although the fluctuating charge and polarization of real systems are neglected in this model, the assigned SPC/E-FW fixed charges adequately capture the long-range behavior of the properties studied here. Furthermore, the understanding of the effect of incorporating long-range interactions gained from this classically based study can be extended to systems of \(a b\) initio nature.

In the DeePMD scheme, system energies and component-wise atomic forces are trained using a deep neural network to represent the potential energy surface of a chosen reference model. The long-range model generated from the decomposition formalism, termed "DPMD-LR," is constructed by decomposition of the training energy and forces to (1) a local non-electrostatic component represented by multidimensional deep neural networks and (2) a pairwise electrostatic component extending to infinite distances. An analogous finite range model, termed "DPMD-FR," is generated for comparison. We show that incorporating long-range
interactions allows us to construct a more physically motivated and transferable model, capable of predicting the properties of systems not included in the training data. Furthermore, our results illustrate how decomposition of electrostatics not only allows for separate inclusion of long-range interactions but also improves the fitting accuracy of the short-range contribution in this model system. In addition to the analysis of isolated cluster properties, a key feature of this work is the extension of the formalism to study thermodynamic properties, such as vapor phase densities and second virial coefficients, of bulk systems in order to provide a practical understanding of the effect of the treatment of longrange interactions in large-scale molecular simulations using ML models.

The structure of this article is as follows: The model development and assessment methods are presented in Sec. II. The analyses of model training errors, isolated cluster properties, and thermodynamic properties are presented in Secs. III A-III C, respectively. Section IV provides a summary of the work.

\section*{II. METHODS}

\section*{A. Model development}

A model following the DeePMD scheme \({ }^{25}\) is constructed in two steps. First, for every configuration in the training set, a local coordinate frame is set up for every atom and its neighbors inside a smooth cutoff radius \(r_{c}\) to allow for translational and rotational symmetries to be preserved. Permutational invariance is preserved by summing over all possible permutation of atoms of the same type. Then, these "descriptor" representations serve as inputs to a deep neural network to undergo linear and nonlinear transformations and output atomic energies \(E_{i}\) for every atom \(i\). The sum of the atomic energies \(E=\sum_{i} E_{i}\) defines the total potential energy of a given configuration, where the additive form of \(E\) naturally preserves the extensive nature of the potential energy surface. Atomic forces \(\mathbf{F}_{i}\) are obtained from the gradient of the system energy. Parameters for the network hidden layers are determined by minimizing the loss function \(L\), defined as a sum of squared errors normalized by the number of atoms,
\[
L=p_{e} \Delta E^{2}+\frac{p_{f}}{3 N} \sum_{i} \Delta \mathbf{F}_{i}^{2}
\]
where \(\Delta E^{2}\) and \(\Delta \mathbf{F}_{i}^{2}\) are the squared errors of system energy and atomic component-wise force, respectively, between the reference model and the DeePMD-constructed model predictions and \(N\) is the number of atoms. The two terms of the loss function are weighted with additional prefactors \(p_{e}\) and \(p_{f}\) to optimize the training efficiency.

High-level density functional theory (DFT) methods have served as the reference model for a number of previously constructed potentials using the DeePMD scheme. In this work, we chose our reference model to be a simple flexible model, termed "SPC/E-FW," in order to provide a well-defined benchmark system and focus our study on the treatment and influence of long-range interactions. The model is modified from the standard rigid SPC/E water model \({ }^{7}\) with bond-stretching constant \(k_{b} /=824.5698 \mathrm{kcal} /\left(\mathrm{mol} / \AA^{2}\right)\) and angle-bending constant \(k_{a} =91.5390 \mathrm{kcal} /\left(\mathrm{mol} / \mathrm{rad}^{2}\right)\), parameters of flexible SPC \(/\) E water, as
defined in GROMACS. \({ }^{54}\) The short-range van der Waals interactions are described by a truncated and shifted Lennard-Jones potential in order to simplify the model such that the only remaining long-range interaction is the standard pairwise Coulombic interaction implemented with the particle-particle particle-mesh (PPPM) solver. \({ }^{55}\)

Configurations for the training set were obtained by sampling
1. bulk liquid states at \(\mathrm{T}=300-500 \mathrm{~K}\) of 64 molecules in periodic systems obtained using molecular dynamics simulations in the isothermal-isobaric (NPT) ensemble,
2. isolated clusters of 27 water molecules (to target interfacial properties), and
3. additional condensed phase representations following an active learning method developed by Zhang et al., \({ }^{46}\) where atomic configurations are selected from an iterative scheme to provide a computationally efficient exploration of the configurational phase space.

The total training set is comprised of system energies, atomic component-wise forces, and coordinates from 1525 chosen configurations, and the local environment cutoff \(r_{c}\) is chosen to be 6 Å.

To incorporate long-range electrostatics into the DeePMD framework, we utilize a formalism to decompose the total interaction energies and forces into two components: pairwise electrostatic interactions and non-electrostatic interactions, as illustrated schematically in Fig. 1. The electrostatic contributions follow the standard Coulombic potential and are calculated using fixed SPC/EFW charges between all atom-atom pairwise interactions. While in this work the electrostatic interactions are obtained using fixed charges that reproduce the long-range electrostatic field of the isolated molecule in its ground state, the same approach can apply for \(a b\) initio based models for which the instantaneous charges depend on the local environment. The non-electrostatic interactions, such as bond, angle, and van der Waals interactions, are incorporated through a DeePMD constructed potential. The remaining non-electrostatic interaction energies and forces make up the training set for the neural network potential. The resultant potential is combined with explicit pairwise electrostatic interactions to give the final long-range model DPMD-LR.

In this manner, we take advantage of the multidimensional capacity of the neural network to fit the complex local representation, while the classical pairwise electrostatics are separately induced
and can be extended to infinite distances utilizing long-range solvers. The same configurations are used to train both the DPMD-FR and DPMD-LR models in order to compare and analyze the effect of the long-range representation.

\section*{B. Model assessment}

All simulations described in this section were performed using the LAMMPS molecular dynamics package \({ }^{56}\) interfaced with the DeePMD-kit. \({ }^{57}\)

\section*{1. Isolated cluster properties}

Single point calculations of isolated water clusters were performed for the SPC/E-FW, DPMD-FR, and DPMD-LR models. All analyses feature molecules in fixed positions, while a single molecule is moved along a specified metric (displacement or rotation). Pairwise electrostatic interactions were implemented in a nonperiodic box with a cutoff encompassing all molecules. Intramolecular interactions were subtracted from the total energy to generate the intermolecular potential energy curve. The vibrational modes of a 10 -water cluster were obtained from the eigenvalues of a mass-weighted Hessian of the potential energy \({ }^{58}\) using a step size of \(0.01 \AA\).

\section*{2. Bulk densities and diffusion coefficients}

The liquid density isobar at \(\mathrm{P}=100\) bars and the vapor density isotherm at \(\mathrm{T}=600 \mathrm{~K}\) were obtained by isobaric-isothermal MD simulations, performed with a system of 64 water molecules using the SPC/E-FW, DPMD-FR, and DPMD-LR models. Temperature and pressure control was maintained by the Nosé-Hoover thermostat and barostat, \({ }^{59}\) respectively. Liquid diffusion coefficients at \(\mathrm{P}=100\) bars were obtained from the mean squared displacement of molecules in MD simulations in the canonical ensemble with a system of 256 water molecules. Classical equations of motion were numerically integrated with a time step of 0.5 fs . Simulations consisted of 500 ps equilibration runs, followed by 2 ns production runs.

\section*{3. Vapor-liquid equilibrium properties}

The vapor-liquid equilibrium properties predicted by the SPC/E-FW, DPMD-FR, and DPMD-LR models were calculated by

