\includegraphics[alt={},max width=\textwidth]{https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/3a8ffbb4-be01-45ba-9239-780f86adb541-04.jpg?height=300&width=1081&top_left_y=1980&top_left_x=494}
\captionsetup{labelformat=empty}
\caption{FIG. 1. A schematic of the decomposition of the total system energy to electrostatic and non-electrostatic components. The non-electrostatic component is used to train a DeePMD potential. The final long-range potential, DPMD-LR, then combines the DeePMD trained potential with explicit pairwise Coulombic interactions extending to infinite distances.}
\end{figure}
direct coexistence interfacial MD simulations. To prepare the initial configuration, an isobaric-isothermal MD simulation of liquid water in a cubic box was performed until equilibration, followed by an expansion of the box size along the \(z\)-axis. The generated interfacial system consisted of 512 water molecules in a box with dimensions of \(25 \times 25 \times 90 \AA^{3}\). The interfacial simulations were then performed in the canonical ensemble with temperature control maintained by the Nosé-Hoover thermostat. For all three models, simulations consisted of a 1 ns equilibration run, followed by a 4 ns production run. Simulations using the SPC/E-FW and DPMD-LR models included long-range electrostatic interactions handled by the PPPM solver with the relative error in forces smaller than \(10^{-6}\). Liquid equilibrium densities were obtained by fitting the density profile with the hyperbolic tangent function, given by
\[
\rho(z)=\frac{\rho_{l}+\rho_{v}}{2}-\frac{\rho_{l}-\rho_{v}}{2} \tanh \left(\frac{z-z_{0}}{d}\right)
\]
where \(\rho_{l}\) and \(\rho_{v}\) are the densities of the liquid and vapor phases, respectively, \(z_{0}\) is the position of the interface, and \(d\) is the interfacial thickness. Vapor densities were calculated by averaging the vapor phase density profiles obtained from simulations.

\section*{4. Second virial coefficients}

Virial coefficients are important thermodynamic quantities that indicate the deviation of a dense fluid system from the ideal gas state. The second virial coefficient is related to the pairwise interaction between two molecules and is calculated from the following definition:
\[
B_{2}(T)=-2 \pi \int_{0}^{\infty}\left(\left\langle e^{-U / k_{B} T}-1\right\rangle\right) r^{2} d r
\]
where \(U\) is the pairwise energy, \(e^{-U / k_{B} T}-1\) is the Mayer function, and \(r\) is the distance between the molecules' centers of mass (COMs). For a flexible model, \(U\) is not only a function of \(r\) but also a function of the geometries of both molecules and relative orientations between them. Therefore, the brackets indicate the average of the Mayer function with respect to different molecule geometries and relative orientations.

Different molecule geometries (with different bond lengths and angles) were obtained from MD simulations of water in the vapor phase at temperatures ranging from 373 K to 650 K . These pairs of molecules were subsequently put into 30000 randomly sampled different relative orientations at each intermolecular distance to calculate the average Mayer function. The radial integration was performed with the trapezoid rule, spanning a COM-COM distance from \(2.2 \AA\) to \(20.1 \AA\), using 896 points. Three independent sets of relative molecule orientations were used to estimate error bars.

\section*{III. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION}

\section*{A. Training accuracy}

We begin by assessing the effect of the long-range decomposition scheme on the fitting accuracy of two models: DPMD-FR
using the standard DeePMD scheme and DPMD-LR with the long-range decomposition formalism. Short-range contributions for both models are defined by atom-centered local representations of interactions within a given cutoff. For the DPMD-FR model, these representations are fit to configurational total energies and component-wise atomic forces that extend past the cutoff. Following the decomposition formalism, the DPMD-LR model is trained on energies and forces where both the short-range and long-range electrostatic contributions are removed. Figure 2 shows the learning curves of the DPMD-FR model and non-electrostatic component of the DPMD-LR model, where the squared energy and force errors are defined in Eq. (1). The fitting error of DPMD-LR for both the energies and forces is an order of magnitude lower than that of DPMD-FR. For the DPMD-FR model constructed from the standard DeePMD scheme, the additional long-range component cannot be directly mapped to configurational coordinates. This lack of specificity inhibits the flexibility of the fitting, and the inclusion of this additional long-range component "contaminates" the short-range mapping and hinders the fitting accuracy of the local representation. For the DPMD-LR model constructed from the decomposition formalism, the removal of the shortrange "contamination" allows for the potential fitting to achieve a higher threshold of accuracy. We further assess this feature with a

