supplementary test set comprised of 900 independent configurations of liquid water. Figure 3(a) shows the probability density of absolute DPMD-FR/DPMD-LR deviation of atomic forces from the reference SPC/E-FW model in this test set. The significantly narrower distribution for DPMD-LR similarly shows a more precise and accurate fitting due to the removal of the short-range "contamination," showcasing a key drawback of the local representation scheme. In Fig. 3(b), we showcase an additional measurement of the magnitude of the long-range fluctuations in our reference SPC/E-FW system, which corresponds to the same "contamination," following the procedures of Deringer and Csányi \({ }^{60}\) and Fujikake et al. \({ }^{61}\) We find the magnitude of the long-range fluctuations for a cutoff of \(6 \AA\) to be comparable to the size "contamination" from the force errors in Figs. 2 and 3(a).

It remains to be seen if improvement in fitting accuracy from removing short-range "contamination" will be as significant for \(a b\) initio based reference systems, where short-range electrostatic contributions are represented by spatially delocalized electrons, which differ from the point charges used in this work. Future investigations will assess the magnitude of fitting accuracy in \(a b\) initio based systems using smoother Gaussian charges.

\section*{B. Cluster analysis}

We now examine our long-range implementation on a simple system of pairwise intermolecular interactions between two molecules. These systems were not included in the training set. Figure 4 shows the potential energy of isolated water cluster defined by the two models, DPMD-FR and DPMD-LR, compared with the reference SPC/E-FW model. Unsurprisingly, the locally defined DPMD-FR model with its many-body nature deviates from the two-body SPC/E-FW reference model at short distances. The key

