The water molecular pair analysis showcases the improved transferability and generalizability features of excluding shortranged "contamination" in the local representation and incorporating explicit long-range interactions. Although trained only on bulk liquid and interface systems, the DPMD-LR model is able to extend to isolated cluster properties, driven by physically motivated functional forms. Furthermore, the water molecular pair analysis provides a preview of the effect of longrange interactions in the representation of the vapor phase since vapor interactions are dominated by two-body intermolecular interactions.

A more stringent test of the effects of long-range interactions in many-body representations is the vibrational normal modes calculated from the eigenvalues of the mass-weighted Hessian, as shown in Fig. 5. We find that the local representation of the DPMD-FR model is capable of accurately representing the intramolecular stretching and bending modes but shows deficiencies for the intermolecular translational and rotational modes. This suggests that the benefit of long-range incorporation is isolated to the intermolecular interactions rather than intramolecular.

\section*{C. Thermodynamic properties}

\section*{1. Liquid and vapor densities}

