\section*{IV. CONCLUSIONS}

ML models have proven to be highly successful at representing complex many-body interactions for a multitude of chemical systems. These frameworks are often based on atom-centered local representations where interactions are truncated at a certain cutoff distance. In this work, we explore the limitations of this approximation by applying the DeePMD scheme on a simple system of SPC/EFW water to systematically study the influence of the treatment of long-range interactions in systems of isolated clusters, liquid, and vapor phase behavior. We find that for many of these properties, long-range interactions are important, which has implications for the future development of ML models. We employ a formalism for atomistic ML models to incorporate explicit pairwise long-range electrostatics by decomposition of energy and forces in the training set into electrostatic and non-electrostatic contributions. This formalism does not require the assignment of instantaneous charges that depend on the local configuration, but rather any set of charges that capture the long-range electrostatic field of isolated molecules. Our assessment provides insight toward the regimes where explicit long-range interactions for machine-learning models are necessary.

In our analysis of training errors, we find a key deficiency of the local representation scheme where the inclusion of longrange energies and forces "contaminates" the short-range local representation fitting for our model system. The effects of removing "contamination" and including long-range interactions are shown to improve predictions of cluster potential energy surfaces and vibrational normal modes. We then probed the treatment of longrange interactions in properties of bulk liquid and vapor phase water. Vapor phase properties, such as densities and second virial coefficients, are strongly affected by the lack of explicit longrange electrostatics as expected. Liquid phase structure and dynamics, however, can be predicted without explicit long-range electrostatics, likely due to strong screening effects in the condensed phase. Liquid-vapor equilibrium densities are also accurately predicted without explicit inclusion of long-range electrostatics. We find, for bulk properties of water, explicit long-range electrostatics are necessary for densities above ideal gas and below the liquid phase.

It is also important to note that adding long-range electrostatic interactions on top of the model generated by the DeePMD procedure results in nearly \(2 \times\) higher computational cost. Using the same computational resources, a single Xeon Broadwell E5-2680 v4 node with 2.4 GHz and four 1328 MHz NVIDIA P100 GPUs, 28 Intel Broadwell e5-2680v4 CPU cores, a simulation of a 64 -molecules condensed phase system performed with DPMD-FR has a speed of
\(9.5 \mathrm{~ns} /\) day, while the same simulation with DPMD-LR presents a speed of \(5.0 \mathrm{~ns} /\) day .

While we have chosen here to use SPC/E-FW as the reference model and DeePMD as the ML scheme, we anticipate the understanding of long-range inclusion/exclusion can be generalized and applied to \(a b\) initio reference models and other ML schemes. The long-range decomposition formalism, in principle, can also be extended to \(a b\) initio reference systems using static charges calculated from the gas-phase dipole moment for calculating large-scale molecular fluid properties. While fixed point charges are clearly a gross simplification of the dynamically fluctuating, diffuse charges in \(a b\) initio datasets and for real systems, they could be adequate for certain properties influenced primarily by long-range interactions, e.g., properties at relatively low densities. Moreover, a more realistic representation of fluctuating charge and polarization is feasible with neural networks for the Wannier centers: \({ }^{62}\) How to use this approach to include long-range electrostatics in ML models is the subject of ongoing work.

Additionally, the insights gained from this work can be extended to various other chemical systems. Future investigations will address charged electrolyte systems, which contain even stronger long-range interactions than pure water, and ice phases, where long-range effects are likely more pronounced since screening effects are significantly reduced relative to liquid water. Another interesting question for future work would be to examine more closely the origin of the good representation of bulk structure and dynamics as well as phase equilibrium properties by a ML model that lacks long-range interactions. Since, in the present work, a rather generic model for a molecular liquid was examined, we feel that the most likely explanation is the screening of the long-range interactions because of the rapid decay of correlations at long distances in a normal fluid away from a critical point.

